81e74cbc2cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42663" :question/problem "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006797/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the value $3\\pi $ is not the range of the function." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-42663/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42663"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65939"] :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006797/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-65947"] :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-117218/selection/b937b5a9c9148a5914243385a1d84bacb019d6c5ff771ce01182a0f3091b9285" :answer/id #uuid "8f97563a-fac7-58b9-a3be-064d0e5ce97e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117218/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "be9d7e17-ecd0-53f6-bcda-09614300b847" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-117218/selection/0d474ca756c76f6ecddcd5fcfb411f0292ef0ce725af778ee27af0d5dc8595bc" :answer/id #uuid "72449ce8-8ff0-5e25-b8ee-af6a5ca7e305" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answ